.DS_Store
logs/
# runs/experiments.jsonl (the research trial log) stays gitignored -- it is
# a large, purely local research artifact (see research/experiment_log.py's
# DEFAULT_RUNS_PATH docstring). runs/live_signals.jsonl is the opposite: a
# low-volume, once-daily operational audit trail for the daily-tsmom-ensemble
# paper-trading signal runner (python/live/generate_daily_signal.py) that is
# deliberately git-committed, same treatment as any other operational log --
# see .planning/paper-trading-b-signal-runner.md. `runs/*` (not `runs/`)
# is required for the negation below to take effect -- git does not descend
# into a directory excluded via a bare trailing-slash pattern.
runs/*
!runs/live_signals.jsonl
# runs/spent_windows.json is the same shape: a small COMMITTED artifact
# derived from the gitignored experiments log by research/spent_windows.py,
# so CI can check CLAUDE.md's "unspent window" claim against something
# real. Without it that check passes vacuously wherever the log is absent.
!runs/spent_windows.json
# runs/discovery_windows.json is committed for the same reason and is NOT
# derived: a discovery analysis is not a backtest run, so nothing writes it to
# the experiments log and the derived ledger above cannot see it. Without this
# file a window spent in discovery mode reads as available for confirmation,
# which is the stale claim that check exists to catch.
!runs/discovery_windows.json
# runs/krx_futures_liquidity.json is committed for a different reason than
# either of the above: it is the ONLY copy. KIS drops an expired
# single-stock futures contract's entire series -- measured 2026-09-16,
# expiries 2026-01 and later answer and 2025-12 and earlier return nothing
# at all -- so the front-month history this universe was selected from
# stops being fetchable within months and takes the selection's
# reproducibility with it. ~1.9MB of raw bars rather than only the
# medians, because a different question needs the bars. See
# .planning/rd-r-futures-liquidity-universe.md §3.3.
!runs/krx_futures_liquidity.json
# runs/krx_dayone_universe.json is committed for a THIRD reason, and the
# distinction matters because the obvious one does not apply: the 2019-01
# bars it ranks on are stable and KIS serves them indefinitely, so unlike
# the futures file above this is not the only copy of decaying data. What
# cannot be reconstructed is the CANDIDATE POOL -- it is today's live
# universe plus today's delisted list, and the delisted list only grows,
# so a re-run in a year ranks a larger pool and may return a different
# thirty. A point-in-time selection whose inputs move is not reproducible
# by re-running it. ~450KB: the chosen thirty plus all 2,180 ranked
# candidates, the second so a later reader can check the ranking rather
# than trust it. See .planning/rd-x-the-day-one-universe.md §3.
!runs/krx_dayone_universe.json
python/data/var/
# ...and the same store wherever a wrong working directory puts it. On
# 2026-09-10 a `cd python && PYTHONPATH=.` invocation resolved
# DEFAULT_DB_PATH ("python/data/var/klines.sqlite3") relative to `python/`
# itself, creating `python/python/data/var/` -- which the anchored rule
# above does not match, so a 36K SQLite file reached main via a `git add
# -A`. Unanchored, so depth cannot defeat it again.
**/data/var/
# Locally-generated runtime data for the live paper-trading signal runner
# (python/live/generate_daily_signal.py) -- the signal file(s) it writes,
# mirroring python/data/var/'s existing gitignore treatment for the kline
# cache above.
#
# **Unanchored, for the same reason `**/data/var/` above is** — and the
# anchored form had the same defect, found on 2026-09-22 on the instance.
# A pattern containing a slash is anchored at the repository root, so
# `var/live/` never matched `python/var/live/`, which is exactly where a
# `cd python` invocation puts it: `health_check --dry-run` took its lock
# there and left an untracked `python/var/`, which then tripped
# `check_deployment`'s own `uncommitted_changes` alert. A check firing on
# an artifact of running that check is the kind of false positive that
# gets a check switched off.
# ...which is why the rule is now the whole directory rather than the
# extensions of the artifacts found so far. **Three incidents, one shape**, and
# the third is what made a per-extension rule obviously the wrong instrument:
#
#  1. `2026-09-22` — `health_check --dry-run` took its lock in `python/var/`,
#     and `check_deployment`'s own `uncommitted_changes` alert fired on an
#     artifact of running that check. A check firing on itself is the kind of
#     false positive that gets a check switched off.
#  2. `2026-09-28` — the pre-2019 backfill's log at `var/krx-pre2019.log`
#     blocked the next deploy outright: `scripts/vps-deploy.sh` refuses to run
#     on ANY `git status --porcelain` output, untracked files included.
#  3. `2026-09-30` — `collect-krx-pre2019.sh`'s `flock` file. Caught on review
#     before it reached the instance, and it is a `.lock`, which the `.log`
#     rule that answered (2) does not match.
#
# **Nothing under any `var/` is tracked** (`git ls-files | grep var/` is
# empty), so this loses nothing today, and a file that ever does need
# committing can be un-ignored with a `!` line at that point. The alternative —
# adding an extension per incident — is a blocklist betting on having thought
# of every artifact, which this project has already recorded losing.
#
# **The `**/` is for a reader, not for git** -- and the first draft of this
# comment said otherwise, so the correction is the note worth keeping.
# `var/` and `**/var/` are equivalent: a pattern is anchored at the repository
# root only when it contains a slash somewhere other than the end, so a
# trailing-slash-only pattern matches at every level. Measured: with `var/`
# alone, `python/var/x.lock` IS ignored.
#
# What the anchoring rule really bit in (1) is `var/live/`, whose slash is in
# the middle: that one is anchored and genuinely never matched
# `python/var/live/`. The `**/` prefix stays here for consistency with the
# `**/data/var/` and `**/var/live/` lines above, where it does real work.
**/var/

# The paste-ready external-review bundles, built by
# scripts/build-external-review-paste.py from .planning/xr-*.md. Generated, so
# committing one would let it drift from the documents of record -- and it sits
# at the repository root rather than in .planning/ both so an operator can find
# it among 123 planning documents and so test_planning_index.py does not count
# it as one.
/PASTE-THIS-phase*.md
